{
  "summary": "Standardizes flatfile allocation sizes, chunk sizes, and block storage overhead constants to `uint64_t` instead of `unsigned int` or `size_t`. It updates Windows `AllocateFileRange` to set `LARGE_INTEGER::QuadPart` directly instead of splitting low and high 32-bit parts. Additionally, it distinguishes error strings for `ReadVarInt` overflow checks and adds test coverage for VarInt deserialization overflows.",
  "problem": "File sizes and chunk offsets in flatfile storage were inconsistently typed between `size_t`, `unsigned int`, and `uint64_t`, which is brittle across 32-bit and 64-bit platforms. Furthermore, `ReadVarInt` lacked unit test coverage for integer overflow conditions when reading into narrower types like `uint32_t`.",
  "evidence": [
    "Commit faf8d0e33a notes: 'File sizes are generally measured in 64-bit integers and using 32-bit integers or architecture-dependent integers is brittle and confusing.'",
    "Commit fa2c8df181 notes: 'there is no test coverage at all for VarInt overflow behavior, so add test coverage for both overflow cases (and distinguish their error msg).'"
  ],
